		}, [a, b]);
      * Stable Dependencies in useEffect:
      	- React optimizes certain values to have a stable identity across renders. 
        - This means the reference to the object or function remains the same, even if the component re-renders. 
        - When a dependency has a stable identity, including it in the useEffect dependency array won't cause the effect to re-run unless another, non-stable dependency changes. 
        - You can safely omit them, or include them (as the linter might suggest) without affecting the effect's re-run behavior. 
        - Some dependencies that are always stable:
		1) useRef objects: The object returned by useRef maintains a stable identity throughout the component's lifecycle.
		2) useState setter functions: The functions returned by useState (e.g., setCount) are guaranteed by React to have a stable identity.
		3) useReducer dispatch functions: Similar to useState setters, dispatch functions from useReducer also have stable identities.
	- However, if a ref or similar value is passed from a parent component, you must include it in the dependency array because you cannot guarantee the parent will always pass the same instance.
      * The useEffect clean-up function:
        - The useEffect cleanup function runs not only when the component unmounts, but also before every subsequent re-render where the effect's dependencies have changed (i.e. when the useEffect callback needs to run again).
        - The cleanup function should stop or undo whatever the Effect was doing. 
        - The rule of thumb is that the user shouldn’t be able to distinguish between the Effect running once (as in production) and a setup → cleanup → setup sequence (as you’d see in development).
      * Most of the Effects you’ll write will fit into one of the common patterns below:
