      * In JavaScript, each newly created object and function is considered distinct from all the others. 
      * It doesn’t matter that the contents inside of them may be the same!
      * Objects and functions defined during render (top-level scope of functional component) are created from scratch on every render, resulting in a new instance each time.
      * Object and function dependencies can make your Effect re-synchronize more often than you need.
      * This is why, whenever possible, you should try to avoid objects and functions as your Effect’s dependencies. 
      * To do this,
      	1) Move static objects and functions outside your component
      	2) Move dynamic objects and functions inside your Effect 
      	   - You can write your own functions to group pieces of logic inside your Effect. 
      	   - As long as you also declare them inside your Effect, they’re not reactive values, and so they don’t need to be dependencies of your Effect
        3) Read primitive values from objects 
           - Object may be defined and passed as prop from parent during render, which might cause child's Effect to re-run if the parent re-renders. 
           - To avoid re-running Effect, we can destructure object prop and read primitives from it during child's render.
           - These primitves can then be used directly inside the Effect
        4) Calculate primitive values from functions 
           - Similar to how we read primitives in case of Objects
           - A function passed as prop can also be wrapped using useEffectEvent to make it non-reactive and use it inside the Effect without declaring it as a dependency.
           
